import java.util.Scanner;

public class VowelToNumberConverter {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);
        System.out.print("Enter a string: ");
        String input = scanner.nextLine();

        StringBuilder modifiedString = new StringBuilder();

        for (int i = 0; i < input.length(); i++) {
            char c = input.charAt(i);

            if (Character.isLetter(c)) {
                switch (c) {
                    case 'a':
                        modifiedString.append('1');
                        break;
                    case 'e':
                        modifiedString.append('2');
                        break;
                    case 'i':
                        modifiedString.append('3');
                        break;
                    case 'o':
                        modifiedString.append('4');
                        break;
                    case 'u':
                        modifiedString.append('5');
                        break;
                    case 'y':
                        modifiedString.append('0');
                        break;
                    default:
                        modifiedString.append(c);
                }
            } else {
                modifiedString.append(c);
            }
        }

        System.out.println("Modified string: " + modifiedString.toString());
    }
}
```

This program works by iterating over each character in the input string. If the character is a letter, it checks its value and appends the corresponding number to the `StringBuilder`. If the character is 'y', it appends '0'. If the character is not a letter, it appends the character as-is.

Note that this program does not handle uppercase letters, but you can modify it to handle both cases by checking if the character is uppercase or lowercase using `Character.isUpperCase()` or `Character.isLowerCase()` methods.

